WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.289
Industrial communication hunts compare observed behavior with expected assets, roles,

00:00:04.289 --> 00:00:06.384
protocols, paths, timing, and operations.

00:00:06.384 --> 00:00:12.249
New devices or peers may represent replacement equipment, a vendor laptop, an inventory gap,

00:00:12.249 --> 00:00:13.925
or an unauthorized connection.

00:00:13.925 --> 00:00:16.857
Validate ownership and function before assigning intent.

00:00:16.857 --> 00:00:21.465
Unexpected protocol use can reveal a configuration change or unmanaged pathway.

00:00:21.465 --> 00:00:25.655
Protocol-aware telemetry may also show unusual read and write patterns,

00:00:25.655 --> 00:00:29.844
new engineering sessions, or operations inconsistent with the device role.

00:00:29.844 --> 00:00:34.871
The meaning depends on what the process was doing at the time.

00:00:34.871 --> 00:00:36.966
Controller-to-controller relationships are often stable.

00:00:36.966 --> 00:00:39.060
A new relationship, changed direction,

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:43.669
or communication crossing an architectural zone can guide a focused hunt.

00:00:43.669 --> 00:00:47.858
Compare actual flows with approved conduits, firewall policy, network diagrams,

00:00:47.858 --> 00:00:49.115
and change records.

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:49.953
Timing matters.

00:00:49.953 --> 00:00:52.466
Activity outside production rhythm, maintenance windows,

00:00:52.466 --> 00:00:55.399
or normal polling intervals may deserve review.

00:00:55.399 --> 00:00:58.331
However, startup, failover, batch transitions, equipment faults,

00:00:58.331 --> 00:01:02.101
and communications recovery can also change volume and timing.

00:01:02.101 --> 00:01:04.615
Use passive data and existing records.

00:01:04.615 --> 00:01:07.548
Hunters should not generate writes, discovery traffic,

00:01:07.548 --> 00:01:11.318
or disruptive scans in production to test a theory.

00:01:11.318 --> 00:01:15.507
Correlate network evidence with controller status, historian trends, HMI alarms,

00:01:15.507 --> 00:01:17.602
remote sessions, and engineering activity.

00:01:17.602 --> 00:01:20.953
Document the baseline used, deviations found, operational explanation,

00:01:20.953 --> 00:01:22.210
and remaining uncertainty.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.980
A communication anomaly is a lead, not a verdict.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:31.008
The strongest finding links a meaningful change to asset identity, process context,

00:01:31.008 --> 00:01:33.940
supporting evidence, and a safe escalation path.
